{% if deseq2 and contrasts and datasets %}
Enrichment of the more and less accessible TE peaks of each contrast in
{{ datasets | join_and }} was tested with
{{ tool("gat", number(gat_samples) ~ " samples, random seed 42") }}, with the
genome without {{ filter_chrs | join_and }} and the blacklist as workspace and
q-values over all tests of a contrast. Overlaps were shown as area-proportional
Euler diagrams with {{ tool("eulerr") }}.
{% endif %}

{% if te_only %}
QC of the TE alignments: TSS enrichment as in ENCODE ({{ tss_text() | trim }}),
fragment length distribution and GC bias with
{{ tool("picard", "CollectInsertSizeMetrics and CollectGcBiasMetrics") }} and
SAMtools stats, combined with {{ tool("multiqc") }}.
{% else %}
QC of the TE alignments (TSS enrichment, fragment length distribution, GC bias
and SAMtools stats) was done as above and combined with MultiQC.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}

{% if rnaseq %}
## Integration with RNA-seq

Differential accessibility was compared with RNA-seq DESeq2 results of the same
contrast ({{ rnaseq | join_and }}). Genes were matched to the genome annotation
by gene ID or name, and genes with adjusted p < {{ rnaseq_fdr }} and
{{ rnaseq_lfc_text }} were called up- or downregulated; genes without adjusted
p-value were left out.
Gene rows of the RNA-seq results were compared with the
{% if not te_only %}
consensus peaks
{% if te %}
and, separately, with the peaks of the TE module (genome-wide peaks of the
alignment keeping multimapping reads)
{% endif %}
{% else %}
peaks of the TE module (genome-wide peaks of the alignment keeping
multimapping reads)
{% endif %}
; peaks were assigned to the gene of their nearest TSS (ChIPseeker) within
{{ rnaseq_max }}; peaks without adjusted p-value (not tested by DESeq2) were
left out. Enrichment of differential peaks at up- and downregulated genes was
tested with Fisher's exact test (all tested genes as background; per distance
bin, with bins ending at {{ rnaseq_bins }}), and peak and gene log2 fold changes
were correlated (Spearman). Peak-gene pairs with a significant change in both
(|log2 fold change| > {{ rnaseq_pair_lfc }} in both) were classified as
concordant (more accessible and up, or less accessible and down) or
discordant; the larger of the two adjusted p-values was used as the p-value of
a change in both (intersection-union test {{ cite("berger1982") }}).
{% if te %}
Repeat subfamilies in the RNA-seq results (e.g. TEtranscripts) were compared
with the differential accessibility of their insertions and of the TE peaks
overlapping them (Spearman correlation, Fisher's exact tests, and Wilcoxon
rank-sum tests of the RNA-seq log2 fold changes against unchanged
subfamilies), leaving out subfamilies without adjusted p-value in either data
set.
{% endif %}
P-values of the
{% if te %}
Wilcoxon and
{% endif %}
Fisher's exact tests were adjusted with the
Benjamini–Hochberg method {{ cite("benjamini1995") }}.
{% endif %}

